1
00:00:01,083 --> 00:00:02,251
"Previously on The Mentalist..."

2
00:00:02,376 --> 00:00:05,338
{\an8}Jane has worked out that
Red John is one of seven men.

3
00:00:05,588 --> 00:00:08,674
"But Red John also knows
the seven names on Jane's list."

4
00:00:09,383 --> 00:00:11,177
I'm gonna give you the seven names

5
00:00:11,302 --> 00:00:14,472
and I need you to put a G.P.S. bug
on each of their cell phones.

6
00:00:14,597 --> 00:00:15,932
- The wires are up.
- Okay.

7
00:00:16,057 --> 00:00:20,102
We watch them like hawks.
We log everywhere they go 24/7.

8
00:00:20,228 --> 00:00:22,438
"We have an anonymous tip
directed to your unit."

9
00:00:22,563 --> 00:00:25,691
"A person or persons in distress
at 5570 west Huron."

10
00:00:25,942 --> 00:00:27,276
Brett Partridge is there.

11
00:00:34,283 --> 00:00:36,953
Oh God! Partridge.

12
00:00:37,245 --> 00:00:38,412
Tiger...

13
00:00:39,622 --> 00:00:40,665
Tiger.

14
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:42,833
Just that hammer...
you see that up there?

15
00:00:42,959 --> 00:00:44,752
Is that blood
on the hitting side?

16
00:00:44,877 --> 00:00:46,462
Could be.
I'll call forensics.

17
00:00:46,587 --> 00:00:49,090
Did you see that?
He did not want to go up that ladder.

18
00:00:49,215 --> 00:00:52,051
Didn't the psychiatrist's office say
that Red John was afraid of heights?

19
00:00:55,763 --> 00:00:57,306
Where did you come from?

20
00:00:59,767 --> 00:01:00,893
Give me your hand.

21
00:01:02,228 --> 00:01:04,021
While investigating Red John

22
00:01:04,146 --> 00:01:07,316
homeland security's begun
to suspect the existence

23
00:01:07,441 --> 00:01:12,405
of a powerful secret organization
within California law enforcement.

24
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:15,616
We're looking for partners within the F.B.I.
lower level guys

25
00:01:15,741 --> 00:01:19,078
- that we're sure we can trust.
- That's me.

26
00:01:19,203 --> 00:01:20,871
We should talk some more.
Get in the car.

27
00:01:20,997 --> 00:01:22,540
- Do I have a choice?
- No.

28
00:01:22,665 --> 00:01:24,166
I was wrong about you.
You're not Red John.

29
00:01:24,292 --> 00:01:25,793
You're hunting him,
same as me.

30
00:01:25,918 --> 00:01:29,005
We both want our revenge
and only one of us is going to get it.

31
00:01:29,171 --> 00:01:32,216
- Please tell me who the suspects are.
- No.

32
00:01:32,341 --> 00:01:33,843
You understand I'm gonna
have to torture you.

33
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:37,138
Put your hands in the air!
Jane, you all right?

34
00:01:38,514 --> 00:01:40,891
- Are you gonna finish this thing?
- Yes.

35
00:01:45,646 --> 00:01:48,065
There is a powerful organization
within law enforcement.

36
00:01:48,190 --> 00:01:49,609
It's a lot bigger
than you thought.

37
00:01:49,734 --> 00:01:51,193
It's not just the big boys, either.

38
00:01:51,902 --> 00:01:54,322
They even let lower level guys
like me in.

39
00:02:01,037 --> 00:02:02,788
Agent Kirkland tried to escape.

40
00:02:03,247 --> 00:02:05,416
You had no choice
but to use deadly force.

41
00:02:05,541 --> 00:02:06,709
Do you understand?

42
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:09,253
Tiger tiger.

43
00:02:10,713 --> 00:02:11,797
Tiger tiger.

44
00:02:41,077 --> 00:02:42,161
Hold the elevator.

45
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:46,707
So, dad, can we ride
on the cable car?

46
00:02:47,166 --> 00:02:48,376
Yeah, you bet.

47
00:02:49,335 --> 00:02:51,712
- Then lunch in Chinatown?
- Again?

48
00:02:51,837 --> 00:02:54,507
I like those noodles,
and the dumplings.

49
00:02:55,007 --> 00:02:57,259
Come on, come on, come on...

50
00:03:00,763 --> 00:03:02,515
Whoa! What the heck?!

51
00:03:03,182 --> 00:03:05,643
Hey, how do you think you are?
You can't shove my kid.

52
00:03:06,519 --> 00:03:08,521
Don't run away from me!
Hey! Hey!

53
00:03:09,146 --> 00:03:10,272
You open this door!

54
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:13,234
I'm complaining to the management!
You hear me?

55
00:03:14,694 --> 00:03:16,862
- You all right, Buddy? He get ya? Huh?
- I'm okay.

56
00:03:18,572 --> 00:03:19,782
Mr. Parkman?

57
00:03:20,616 --> 00:03:22,368
Sir, this is hotel security.

58
00:03:25,371 --> 00:03:27,790
Mr. Parkman, I'm gonna have to ask you
to open the door, please.

59
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,627
Mr. Parkman? Sir?

60
00:03:32,086 --> 00:03:33,170
I'm coming in.

61
00:03:38,843 --> 00:03:39,969
Help.

62
00:03:41,595 --> 00:03:43,889
- He stabbed me.
- Who stabbed you?

63
00:03:45,057 --> 00:03:46,308
Mr. Parkman?

64
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:48,436
Oh, Jeez. Mr. Parkman?

65
00:03:49,687 --> 00:03:50,813
Get some help.

66
00:03:52,690 --> 00:03:58,195
Call 9-1-1. Room 8-1-5.
Deceased white male.

67
00:04:01,824 --> 00:04:03,659
And you say the victim
appeared worried?

68
00:04:04,076 --> 00:04:05,453
Yeah, he was looking over
his shoulder

69
00:04:05,578 --> 00:04:08,956
when he got in the elevator,
like he was being chased.

70
00:04:09,957 --> 00:04:13,544
- Yeah. He had something in a paper bag.
- Yeah.

71
00:04:13,669 --> 00:04:16,547
- And what sort of time was this?
- 10:55.

72
00:04:17,214 --> 00:04:20,676
I entered the room at 10:58 P.M.
the victim, Chad Parkman

73
00:04:20,801 --> 00:04:22,511
was on the floor
in some distress.

74
00:04:22,762 --> 00:04:25,264
He stated he had been stabbed
by another individual.

75
00:04:25,389 --> 00:04:27,266
Although he appeared to be
alone in the room.

76
00:04:27,391 --> 00:04:29,852
Lisbon. This is the head of hotel security.

77
00:04:29,977 --> 00:04:32,855
A fount of information.
Agent Lisbon from the CBI.

78
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:35,983
- She'll be happy to listen to you.
- Do we have a murder weapon?

79
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:38,194
No, ma'am, nor have I been
able to establish

80
00:04:38,319 --> 00:04:40,112
any means of egress
used by the killer.

81
00:04:40,237 --> 00:04:42,823
Uh, what about a paper bag?
The kid outside mentioned a paper bag.

82
00:04:43,741 --> 00:04:46,619
Just scraps on the toilet.
Must have been shredded and flushed.

83
00:04:46,744 --> 00:04:48,788
- Any idea what was in it?
- No idea.

84
00:04:50,331 --> 00:04:52,166
Maybe the perp who Killed him
took it.

85
00:04:52,416 --> 00:04:54,293
Hey, boss.
Just got a call on the radio.

86
00:04:54,418 --> 00:04:56,921
Another homicide nearby.
Attempted carjacking, looks like.

87
00:04:57,046 --> 00:04:59,381
- Related?
- Probably not. Thought I'd check it out.

88
00:04:59,507 --> 00:05:00,800
All right. Come back
as quick as you can.

89
00:05:00,925 --> 00:05:01,967
Okay.

90
00:05:05,012 --> 00:05:07,473
When I was outside the room,
I heard them struggling

91
00:05:07,723 --> 00:05:10,392
then heard the victim cry out
when he was stabbed.

92
00:05:10,518 --> 00:05:11,894
But then I got the door open...

93
00:05:12,603 --> 00:05:14,522
- he was all alone.
- Window's open.

94
00:05:14,772 --> 00:05:17,399
It's a long way down,
and a long way up to the roof.

95
00:05:17,525 --> 00:05:18,776
I don't think he went that way.

96
00:05:19,193 --> 00:05:21,237
- So, what? Magic?
- Nope.

97
00:05:21,529 --> 00:05:22,571
Just a trick.

98
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:25,241
And we all know how good
you are at those, don't we?

99
00:05:25,491 --> 00:05:26,867
Tricks, I mean.

100
00:05:27,451 --> 00:05:29,078
- Hey, Teresa.
- Ray.

101
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:32,081
What are you doing here?
You don't work at the CBI anymore.

102
00:05:32,206 --> 00:05:34,542
- I'm with a client.
- He works for Visualize.

103
00:05:34,667 --> 00:05:37,628
He does, and this is
a Visualize matter.

104
00:05:38,128 --> 00:05:40,714
- Who are they?
- This is Jason Cooper.

105
00:05:41,006 --> 00:05:43,300
Errand boy for cult leader
Bret Stiles

106
00:05:43,425 --> 00:05:46,011
And Ray Haffner,
who is a member of the cult.

107
00:05:46,136 --> 00:05:48,222
Visualize is not a cult

108
00:05:49,056 --> 00:05:51,350
and it is exactly bigotry like that

109
00:05:51,475 --> 00:05:54,144
- that leads to murders like this.
- What are you talking about?

110
00:05:54,270 --> 00:05:57,565
Chad Parkman was a prominent member
of the Visualize community.

111
00:05:57,773 --> 00:05:59,859
We view his murder
as a hate crime.

112
00:05:59,984 --> 00:06:02,486
Mr. Haffner is to be included
in your investigation.

113
00:06:02,611 --> 00:06:04,446
Purely as a victim's advocate

114
00:06:04,572 --> 00:06:07,157
but I do like the idea of working
with you again, Teresa.

115
00:06:08,367 --> 00:06:10,452
I appreciate your concern,
Mr. Cooper

116
00:06:10,578 --> 00:06:13,789
but we never let civilians
in our investigations... ever.

117
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:16,667
- Never ever?
- Well, he's...

118
00:06:16,792 --> 00:06:18,294
- he's different.
- I'm different.

119
00:06:18,419 --> 00:06:21,297
I have a letter here signed
by the lieutenant governor

120
00:06:21,547 --> 00:06:24,258
Inviting Mr. Haffner
to join the investigation.

121
00:06:24,508 --> 00:06:26,010
To observe. That's all.

122
00:06:26,427 --> 00:06:28,971
Read the letter, agent Lisbon.
I am confident you'll want to grant

123
00:06:29,096 --> 00:06:30,890
the lieutenant governor's request.

124
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:43,777
So... any ideas
on the case yet?

125
00:06:44,278 --> 00:06:46,530
- Just questions.
- It's a stumper, isn't it?

126
00:06:48,073 --> 00:06:50,409
Speaking of which, how's your
Red John investigation going?

127
00:06:50,868 --> 00:06:52,161
In the bag.

128
00:07:00,419 --> 00:07:02,171
You know, we study you
in Visualize now.

129
00:07:02,296 --> 00:07:03,589
- Oh.
- Yeah.

130
00:07:03,714 --> 00:07:05,549
Your blind struggle
against fate.

131
00:07:05,674 --> 00:07:07,509
So heroic. So fruitless.

132
00:07:08,260 --> 00:07:13,098
Bret says there's a lot to be learned
from watching Patrick Jane, so... we all do.

133
00:07:13,766 --> 00:07:15,059
So much anger.

134
00:07:15,601 --> 00:07:18,437
- It's not good for you.
- Anger? I'm not angry.

135
00:07:18,938 --> 00:07:20,522
Annihilating rage.

136
00:07:21,065 --> 00:07:23,025
About what, I have no idea.

137
00:07:23,150 --> 00:07:24,610
You don't know anything about me.

138
00:07:24,902 --> 00:07:26,904
Just what's written on your face.

139
00:07:28,948 --> 00:07:31,200
You're so much smarter
than everybody else, right?

140
00:07:32,368 --> 00:07:34,578
But it isn't helping you
catch Red John, is it?

141
00:07:39,583 --> 00:07:42,503
Here's a switch for you.
I'm gonna predict your future.

142
00:07:43,671 --> 00:07:45,839
There's a comeuppance
headed your way.

143
00:07:49,385 --> 00:07:51,428
You're a worse psychic
than you were a cop.

144
00:08:07,301 --> 00:08:08,343
Hey.

145
00:08:08,468 --> 00:08:10,178
So our victim...
Chad Parkman...

146
00:08:10,304 --> 00:08:12,723
Was a successful Olympic
gymnastics coach.

147
00:08:12,848 --> 00:08:15,100
- He had a facility out in Marin.
- What was he doing at the hotel?

148
00:08:15,225 --> 00:08:17,603
Sports health conference.
He was the keynote speaker.

149
00:08:17,978 --> 00:08:20,188
What about the Visualize angle?
Did you find anything there?

150
00:08:20,314 --> 00:08:22,065
{\an8}Nothing I.D.ing him
as a member.

151
00:08:22,190 --> 00:08:24,484
{\an8}- You think Cooper's lying?
- I don't know.

152
00:08:24,610 --> 00:08:26,862
If Bret stiles sends
his second-in-command

153
00:08:26,987 --> 00:08:30,115
{\an8}and private security, it's important
to Visualize somehow.

154
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:32,659
{\an8}Let's play nice.
I'll go make up with Cooper.

155
00:08:32,910 --> 00:08:35,120
{\an8}I want you to go through
the security tapes with Haffner.

156
00:08:35,245 --> 00:08:38,832
{\an8}He likes you. Just be helpful
and accommodating. Get him talking.

157
00:08:39,082 --> 00:08:41,335
{\an8}Pump him for every bit of information
that you can.

158
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:43,712
{\an8}- Sure, boss. I know what to do.
- But be careful.

159
00:08:44,212 --> 00:08:45,464
- You know.
- Yeah.

160
00:08:48,842 --> 00:08:50,886
Hey. You wanna come with me
to talk to Cooper?

161
00:08:51,011 --> 00:08:53,513
Nope.
We don't want to spook him.

162
00:08:53,931 --> 00:08:55,641
I'll take Cho to Parkman's gym.

163
00:08:55,766 --> 00:08:59,269
He can talk reps and curls
and I'll take a little look around.

164
00:08:59,394 --> 00:09:01,438
All right. I'll call you
if I find anything out.

165
00:09:18,205 --> 00:09:20,332
{\an8}"That's right. Point your toes, perfect."

166
00:09:24,628 --> 00:09:27,130
{\an8}Nice... Nice...

167
00:09:30,175 --> 00:09:32,552
{\an8}All right, now...
Stick the landing, Kimmy.

168
00:09:33,220 --> 00:09:34,554
{\an8}Good...

169
00:09:36,515 --> 00:09:37,683
That's right!

170
00:09:38,558 --> 00:09:40,560
Wow. She's impressive.

171
00:09:40,811 --> 00:09:42,980
You know, Rio 2016...
that's the goal.

172
00:09:43,689 --> 00:09:46,108
{\an8}And, Parkman was
your first choice as coach?

173
00:09:46,233 --> 00:09:49,778
{\an8}You bet. Parkman
offering to coach your kid?

174
00:09:50,112 --> 00:09:52,072
{\an8}Ah, you know, he's probably
too busy with Kimmy

175
00:09:52,197 --> 00:09:54,866
{\an8}to take your kid on,
but, uh, he's the best.

176
00:09:54,992 --> 00:09:56,201
{\an8}- Yeah?
- Oh, yeah.

177
00:09:56,326 --> 00:09:58,120
He got Kimmy to bear down
in ways I never could.

178
00:09:58,245 --> 00:09:59,496
She's always texting
with friends

179
00:09:59,621 --> 00:10:02,082
{\an8}wanting to go for pizzas
on a Friday night with boys.

180
00:10:02,207 --> 00:10:03,625
{\an8}You know? No more of that.

181
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:05,002
- Thank goodness.
- Yeah. Yeah.

182
00:10:05,127 --> 00:10:08,839
{\an8}And I never felt I have to worry
about her being alone with him.

183
00:10:09,172 --> 00:10:10,674
Well, that would be important,
wouldn't it?

184
00:10:10,799 --> 00:10:13,385
{\an8}- Yeah. All right, let's go.
- Thanks.

185
00:10:17,889 --> 00:10:19,850
Did Mr. Parkman ever talk
about his personal life?

186
00:10:19,975 --> 00:10:23,311
{\an8}No, never. I would ask questions sometimes
just to be polite, you know

187
00:10:23,437 --> 00:10:25,480
and he would just be very vague.

188
00:10:25,605 --> 00:10:26,690
Thank you.

189
00:10:29,317 --> 00:10:31,403
- Excuse me...
- Whoa, careful.

190
00:10:31,945 --> 00:10:33,405
- You all right?
- Yeah.

191
00:10:33,530 --> 00:10:35,115
{\an8}Guess I shouldn't have
worn heels.

192
00:10:36,533 --> 00:10:38,493
{\an8}My little sister's
interested in gymnastics.

193
00:10:38,618 --> 00:10:40,787
{\an8}Could you help explain
this pricing to me?

194
00:10:40,912 --> 00:10:42,581
{\an8}- I don't work here.
- Oh. I...

195
00:10:42,706 --> 00:10:44,499
{\an8}Excuse me.
You're the guy from CBI?

196
00:10:44,624 --> 00:10:47,002
- That's right. Agent Kimball Cho.
- I'm Bill Mylar, co-owner.

197
00:10:47,127 --> 00:10:48,587
{\an8}- We talked on the phone.
- Right.

198
00:10:48,712 --> 00:10:50,881
{\an8}Oh, wait. You're here
investigating something?

199
00:10:51,173 --> 00:10:52,215
A murder.

200
00:10:52,340 --> 00:10:53,842
{\an8}I thought you were gonna come
directly to my office.

201
00:10:53,967 --> 00:10:57,679
{\an8}We decided to talk to some of your
clientele. You don't mind, do you?

202
00:10:57,804 --> 00:11:00,724
{\an8}Anything that helps, but the truth is
everybody here at the gym loved Chad.

203
00:11:01,433 --> 00:11:03,018
{\an8}People always say that
about the dead.

204
00:11:03,143 --> 00:11:06,146
{\an8}Seldom is it true. Is there someplace
private we can talk?

205
00:11:10,275 --> 00:11:14,404
So... You're a policeman. Duh!

206
00:11:14,863 --> 00:11:16,823
{\an8}I'm so sorry.
I'm embarrassed.

207
00:11:17,908 --> 00:11:19,493
{\an8}- Somebody died?
- Yes.

208
00:11:19,951 --> 00:11:21,495
{\an8}Oh, my gosh, a murder?

209
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:23,455
- Did you know Chad Parkman?
- The gym owner?

210
00:11:23,789 --> 00:11:24,956
No. What happened?

211
00:11:26,625 --> 00:11:29,086
{\an8}There's a staff member right over there.
They can tell you about the pricing.

212
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:33,173
Tough guy, huh?

213
00:11:35,759 --> 00:11:36,968
Thanks.

214
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:41,515
{\an8}Excuse me. Could you help me
with this pricing?

215
00:11:42,891 --> 00:11:45,769
{\an8}I'm sorry we got off
on the wrong foot.

216
00:11:45,894 --> 00:11:48,480
{\an8}Now I want you to know that
I am completely committed

217
00:11:48,605 --> 00:11:50,732
{\an8}to finding whoever
killed Mr. Parkman.

218
00:11:51,149 --> 00:11:52,609
Our interests are the same.

219
00:11:52,734 --> 00:11:55,862
In light of that, I was wondering
if you could give me

220
00:11:55,987 --> 00:11:59,282
a little history as to
Mr. Parkman's role in Visualize.

221
00:11:59,407 --> 00:12:02,536
I haven't been able to find any evidence
linking him to the organization.

222
00:12:03,703 --> 00:12:06,623
Chad's eyes were opened
to the truth only recently.

223
00:12:07,332 --> 00:12:08,792
But he was deeply committed.

224
00:12:09,459 --> 00:12:12,295
- We had big plans for him.
- What kind of plans?

225
00:12:12,420 --> 00:12:16,299
Chad Parkman had very positive
name recognition across all quadrants.

226
00:12:16,758 --> 00:12:20,595
The most commonly used word
to describe him was "trustworthy."

227
00:12:21,555 --> 00:12:23,557
So you were gonna use him
for public outreach?

228
00:12:23,682 --> 00:12:25,225
Something like that, yes.

229
00:12:25,350 --> 00:12:27,018
Do you think that's why
someone killed him?

230
00:12:27,144 --> 00:12:28,603
It's a strong possibility.

231
00:12:28,728 --> 00:12:31,648
What was Mr. Parkman's
relationship with Bret Stiles?

232
00:12:32,065 --> 00:12:35,110
Bret Stiles was a guide
and a beacon for Chad

233
00:12:35,944 --> 00:12:38,989
- as he is for all of us.
- And where is Mr. Stiles?

234
00:12:39,656 --> 00:12:43,368
- I can't be certain.
- Really? You have no idea where he is?

235
00:12:44,411 --> 00:12:47,998
It is not necessary for me
to know where he is in physical space.

236
00:12:48,623 --> 00:12:51,376
If he needs me,
he will find me.

237
00:12:53,503 --> 00:12:54,629
Would you excuse me?

238
00:12:56,089 --> 00:12:57,132
Hey, Rigs.

239
00:12:57,507 --> 00:12:59,259
I'm at the carjacking scene.

240
00:13:00,427 --> 00:13:01,970
So the victim here
got shot.

241
00:13:02,095 --> 00:13:05,182
On the seat next to him,
we have a bloody knife.

242
00:13:05,432 --> 00:13:07,726
"Do you think it's the same knife
that was used to kill Parkman?"

243
00:13:07,851 --> 00:13:09,144
Yeah, it could be.

244
00:13:11,146 --> 00:13:12,814
You can see the hotel from here.

245
00:13:14,024 --> 00:13:16,401
- Get that knife to the lab.
- Will do, boss.

246
00:13:16,526 --> 00:13:18,528
- "I'm on my way to C.B.I."
- Okay.

247
00:13:26,620 --> 00:13:29,748
Parkman was the face of the business.
I do the business.

248
00:13:30,415 --> 00:13:32,834
- And how is business?
- Phenomenal.

249
00:13:32,959 --> 00:13:36,296
We're planning on expanding
to the southeast, northeast, every place.

250
00:13:37,130 --> 00:13:38,673
Now I don't know
what we're gonna do.

251
00:13:38,924 --> 00:13:41,718
Well, there are upsides to Parkman's death
let's be honest.

252
00:13:42,010 --> 00:13:44,054
What are you talking about?
You know, that's a very sick thing to say.

253
00:13:44,179 --> 00:13:46,306
Well, think about all the sympathetic
press coverage.

254
00:13:46,431 --> 00:13:47,974
That's free publicity
right there.

255
00:13:48,225 --> 00:13:50,435
Plus the whole Visualize mess
goes away.

256
00:13:50,685 --> 00:13:51,937
What are you talking about?

257
00:13:52,062 --> 00:13:53,688
Well, you've heard
of Visualize, right?

258
00:13:53,813 --> 00:13:56,483
Of course I have. It's that wacko cult.
What does that have to do with anything?

259
00:13:56,608 --> 00:13:59,444
Yeah, well, Parkman was
a member of Visualize.

260
00:14:00,111 --> 00:14:01,696
- You didn't know that?
- No.

261
00:14:05,575 --> 00:14:07,619
- You sure?
- Yes, I'm sure.

262
00:14:12,415 --> 00:14:13,458
Hey.

263
00:14:21,675 --> 00:14:23,843
Tell me, did Chad talk about
Visualize a lot?

264
00:14:24,094 --> 00:14:26,221
Yeah, sure.
He was a member.

265
00:14:26,471 --> 00:14:28,723
Some kind of weird
self-help group.

266
00:14:28,848 --> 00:14:31,935
It's not my thing, but if it helped him coach
all the power to him.

267
00:14:33,103 --> 00:14:35,855
And, Mr. Mylar...
How did he feel about Visualize?

268
00:14:35,981 --> 00:14:38,483
Mylar hated that stuff.
You'd see them arguing about it all the time.

269
00:14:38,608 --> 00:14:40,277
Oh, really?
Hated it, did he?

270
00:14:44,656 --> 00:14:47,200
You got me.
A foolish lie.

271
00:14:47,325 --> 00:14:49,577
The truth...I was mad as hell
about Visualize.

272
00:14:49,995 --> 00:14:52,872
I'm trying to expand our business
he Joins some Moonbat cult?

273
00:14:54,165 --> 00:14:58,420
We fought about it a lot, so now
I feel guilty that we ended on bad terms.

274
00:14:59,629 --> 00:15:03,675
- But I didn't kill him.
- You didn't, did you?

275
00:15:06,261 --> 00:15:07,595
Boss wants us back.

276
00:15:08,972 --> 00:15:10,265
Lisbon, you find something?

277
00:15:10,390 --> 00:15:13,310
The knife that Killed Parkman
at the site of Rigsby's Carjacking.

278
00:15:13,435 --> 00:15:17,063
- What was the Carjacking victim's name?
- Frost. Devin Frost.

279
00:15:17,355 --> 00:15:18,690
Tell me about Devin Frost.

280
00:15:19,190 --> 00:15:21,818
Devin? He used to work here.
He's a good kid.

281
00:15:22,110 --> 00:15:24,612
Interesting news.
The plot thickens.

282
00:15:26,281 --> 00:15:27,490
Plot thickens.

283
00:15:28,033 --> 00:15:30,118
Lisbon?
She's gone.

284
00:15:35,221 --> 00:15:37,473
We're in an alley
about a block from the hotel.

285
00:15:37,765 --> 00:15:38,808
We found a gun.

286
00:15:39,058 --> 00:15:40,601
Is this the gun
used to kill Frost?

287
00:15:40,852 --> 00:15:42,937
Yeah, looks like it.
And there's duct tape on the barrel.

288
00:15:43,229 --> 00:15:44,272
"From the silencer."

289
00:15:44,605 --> 00:15:46,899
Well, get it to ballistics.
Make sure it's a match.

290
00:15:47,024 --> 00:15:48,359
See if we can get
any fingerprints.

291
00:15:49,193 --> 00:15:50,611
Will do. Bye.

292
00:15:52,822 --> 00:15:54,574
- Looks like we've got the gun.
- Yeah?

293
00:15:54,699 --> 00:15:56,742
It's on its way to forensics now,
but it's a pretty sure bet.

294
00:15:56,868 --> 00:15:58,995
Well, as soon as Haffner gets here,
we'll get started.

295
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:00,621
I don't like it, boss.

296
00:16:00,872 --> 00:16:02,498
Haffner shouldn't
even be in our offices.

297
00:16:02,623 --> 00:16:04,041
He's one of Jane's
Red John suspects.

298
00:16:04,167 --> 00:16:05,460
And he's a member
of Visualize.

299
00:16:05,585 --> 00:16:07,211
Which is run by another
Red John suspect.

300
00:16:07,336 --> 00:16:09,589
We should be investigating him.
Not letting him join our investigation.

301
00:16:09,714 --> 00:16:13,259
We don't really have a choice.
Might as well make the most of it. Haffner!

302
00:16:13,676 --> 00:16:14,719
Teresa.

303
00:16:17,388 --> 00:16:18,639
Lovely as always.

304
00:16:20,933 --> 00:16:24,562
- Guys. So what have we got so far?
- Well, let's see.

305
00:16:27,148 --> 00:16:29,233
Parkman entered the hotel at 10:48.

306
00:16:29,358 --> 00:16:33,946
He was anxious, and witnesses got
the impression that he was being pursued.

307
00:16:34,530 --> 00:16:36,199
He has an altercation in the elevator

308
00:16:36,324 --> 00:16:39,911
and Parkman flees to his room
at approximately 10:51.

309
00:16:40,161 --> 00:16:43,581
And at 10:58, hotel security
opened Parkman's room

310
00:16:43,706 --> 00:16:46,000
to find him dying on the floor
with a stab wound.

311
00:16:46,125 --> 00:16:48,377
At 12 past 11:00,
police and E.M.T.s arrive

312
00:16:48,503 --> 00:16:50,630
but they pronounce Parkman
dead at the scene.

313
00:16:50,755 --> 00:16:52,215
Nobody could have entered
or left the room

314
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:54,133
during that time period
without being seen.

315
00:16:56,385 --> 00:16:57,595
Here's the park.

316
00:16:58,638 --> 00:17:00,097
The hotel's on the one side

317
00:17:00,223 --> 00:17:02,391
and this is where Devin Frost
was murdered...

318
00:17:02,517 --> 00:17:03,851
On the other side of the park.

319
00:17:03,976 --> 00:17:06,729
Now, in front's car, there's a knife
with Parkman's blood on the blade

320
00:17:06,854 --> 00:17:08,356
and Frost's fingerprints
on the handle.

321
00:17:08,606 --> 00:17:10,691
We also found
a homemade silencer in the car.

322
00:17:10,816 --> 00:17:13,903
And we found the gun that belongs
to the silencer here in the alley

323
00:17:14,028 --> 00:17:16,739
which, given the timeline,
seems to be a physical impossibility.

324
00:17:16,864 --> 00:17:19,742
So... Frost stabbed Parkman

325
00:17:20,243 --> 00:17:23,371
and somehow left the hotel
without being seen

326
00:17:23,621 --> 00:17:26,040
and then an unknown assailant
killed Frost?

327
00:17:26,165 --> 00:17:28,876
It would look that way.
The silencer didn't work too well.

328
00:17:29,001 --> 00:17:31,504
We have three witnesses
who heard a gunshot around 10:57.

329
00:17:31,754 --> 00:17:35,550
So Frost got shot
in his car at almost the exact same time

330
00:17:35,675 --> 00:17:38,386
that he was supposedly
in the hotel stabbing Parkman?

331
00:17:38,511 --> 00:17:40,513
- Which is impossible.
- Correct.

332
00:17:41,055 --> 00:17:43,349
So, you're looking
for an unknown suspect

333
00:17:43,474 --> 00:17:48,229
who is a magician or quite possibly
a wizard of some kind.

334
00:17:49,814 --> 00:17:50,856
Great work, guys.

335
00:17:50,982 --> 00:17:52,858
We've brought in Mylar,
the owner of the gym.

336
00:17:52,984 --> 00:17:54,819
We're hoping he can shed
some light on this

337
00:17:54,944 --> 00:17:56,988
or at least give us
a plausible motive.

338
00:17:57,863 --> 00:18:01,117
Okay then. Well, I'm so glad we got
the "A" team working this case.

339
00:18:01,617 --> 00:18:03,995
I'm gonna go back to the hotel
and look things over again.

340
00:18:04,912 --> 00:18:05,997
Ciao, kids.

341
00:18:13,087 --> 00:18:15,548
Jane said not to Lean on Mylar too hard.

342
00:18:15,673 --> 00:18:17,341
Just ask him about
Parkman and Frost

343
00:18:17,466 --> 00:18:18,926
- and see how he reacts.
- You got it.

344
00:18:19,885 --> 00:18:21,262
Hey, sorry to interrupt.

345
00:18:21,387 --> 00:18:23,014
Weird report just
came over my desk.

346
00:18:23,139 --> 00:18:24,265
It's about Bob Kirkland.

347
00:18:24,390 --> 00:18:26,267
What, the psychotic homeland
security agent we arrested?

348
00:18:26,392 --> 00:18:27,810
Yeah. He's dead.

349
00:18:29,145 --> 00:18:31,188
Dead? How'd he die?

350
00:18:31,314 --> 00:18:33,733
The report says he was Killed
attempting to escape from prison.

351
00:18:33,858 --> 00:18:34,984
Seriously?

352
00:18:35,109 --> 00:18:37,361
- Doesn't sound right, does it?
- No, it doesn't.

353
00:18:37,486 --> 00:18:38,529
I should check it out, right?

354
00:18:38,654 --> 00:18:40,948
- Find out what happened and why.
- Will do, boss.

355
00:18:41,073 --> 00:18:43,075
- And see what we can get from Mylar.
- You got it.

356
00:18:47,747 --> 00:18:49,957
How long had Devin Frost
worked for you at the gym?

357
00:18:50,082 --> 00:18:51,125
Couple years.

358
00:18:51,250 --> 00:18:52,877
What was his relationship
with Chad Parkman?

359
00:18:53,002 --> 00:18:55,212
Parkman had coached him
and brought him on as an assistant.

360
00:18:55,338 --> 00:18:59,592
- Was Frost a member of Visualize?
- Him? I don't think so.

361
00:18:59,717 --> 00:19:02,595
One thing about those Visualize nuts...
They got a work ethic.

362
00:19:02,762 --> 00:19:04,013
And Frost not so much?

363
00:19:08,601 --> 00:19:10,686
- Is that why you fired him?
- I didn't. He quit.

364
00:19:12,730 --> 00:19:15,650
- Really? When?
- About a month ago.

365
00:19:15,775 --> 00:19:17,860
Parkman went to a gymnastics
tournament in Europe.

366
00:19:17,985 --> 00:19:20,279
In the past, Frost went with him.
This time, he didn't.

367
00:19:20,488 --> 00:19:22,782
Instead he walked in, told me
he was quitting, walked out.

368
00:19:24,075 --> 00:19:25,868
- Last time I saw him.
- He and Parkman get into a fight?

369
00:19:25,993 --> 00:19:29,372
No, uh... I just figured he got
a better offer somewhere else

370
00:19:29,497 --> 00:19:32,291
and was taking advantage of Parkman
not being around to quit to me.

371
00:19:32,416 --> 00:19:33,459
Why would he do that?

372
00:19:33,584 --> 00:19:36,796
Like I said, Parkman had been
Frost's mentor since he was a teenager.

373
00:19:37,254 --> 00:19:39,715
I don't think he had the courage
to quit to Parkman's face.

374
00:19:42,718 --> 00:19:45,221
- You think Mylar is our killer?
- Eh.

375
00:19:45,471 --> 00:19:46,597
Me, neither.

376
00:19:47,306 --> 00:19:50,810
Cooper was awfully cagey with you
about stiles' whereabouts, wasn't he?

377
00:19:51,852 --> 00:19:56,023
After I spoke to Cooper,
I did some digging. Stiles is missing.

378
00:19:56,148 --> 00:19:57,608
- Nobody knows where he is.
- Really? When?

379
00:19:57,733 --> 00:20:00,736
Around the same time Red John sent you
The Lorelei Martins tape.

380
00:20:01,404 --> 00:20:04,240
Boss? I just got Frost's phone records.
Look at this.

381
00:20:04,907 --> 00:20:06,409
That's Ray Haffner's cell phone.

382
00:20:06,659 --> 00:20:08,994
Frost called it three times
the week before he stabbed Parkman.

383
00:20:09,453 --> 00:20:12,623
Well, let's go see what brother Ray
has to say about that.

384
00:20:15,501 --> 00:20:18,295
He's not here. He went to the hotel.
He said he had to check on something.

385
00:20:18,421 --> 00:20:21,173
- Then we'll go there.
- No, you stay here. Grace and I will go.

386
00:20:21,298 --> 00:20:23,259
- Don't be ridiculous.
- We'll be fine.

387
00:20:23,384 --> 00:20:26,178
He's more likely to let something slip to us
than he would to you.

388
00:20:26,303 --> 00:20:29,140
All right. But what am I supposed to do
just sit around?

389
00:20:29,265 --> 00:20:31,892
Well, we still haven't figured out
how Frost got out of the hotel room

390
00:20:32,017 --> 00:20:33,227
after he stabbed Parkman.

391
00:20:33,352 --> 00:20:35,938
Except Frost couldn't have been there
in the first place, could he?

392
00:20:36,063 --> 00:20:38,482
You said it was a trick.
Figure it out.

393
00:20:42,278 --> 00:20:44,822
I got the name of the sac P.D. detective
who investigated Kirkland's shooting.

394
00:20:44,947 --> 00:20:46,866
- He's gonna come by CBI.
- Good work.

395
00:20:49,034 --> 00:20:50,494
Hey, man. Usual, please.

396
00:20:51,036 --> 00:20:53,789
- You want anything?
- Yeah, coffee. Black.

397
00:20:54,790 --> 00:20:57,084
It's a fruit stand, Cho.
They sell fruit.

398
00:20:58,461 --> 00:20:59,754
Okay. No.

399
00:21:00,296 --> 00:21:03,007
Give me another one
with everything... lime, chili.

400
00:21:04,884 --> 00:21:05,926
That's great.

401
00:21:06,594 --> 00:21:07,678
Keep the change.

402
00:21:11,265 --> 00:21:12,475
I'm starving.

403
00:21:14,727 --> 00:21:17,938
I guess you burn a lot of calories
being a newlywed.

404
00:21:18,814 --> 00:21:21,150
- We're not having this conversation.
- It's amazing.

405
00:21:21,275 --> 00:21:22,818
You'd think that marriage
would just cool things off

406
00:21:22,943 --> 00:21:24,737
but for us,
it's just the opposite.

407
00:21:25,196 --> 00:21:26,363
Please stop talking.

408
00:21:27,072 --> 00:21:29,158
You know, I think I'm
the happiest I've ever been.

409
00:21:29,492 --> 00:21:30,951
Every morning when I wake up
next to her

410
00:21:31,076 --> 00:21:32,119
and every night when we go
to bed together

411
00:21:32,244 --> 00:21:34,622
I just think, "wow, I'm the luckiest
man in the world", you know?

412
00:21:35,998 --> 00:21:38,334
- I just lost my appetite.
- So I wanna say thank you.

413
00:21:39,376 --> 00:21:40,878
'cause you were the one
that made me take the risk.

414
00:21:41,003 --> 00:21:44,507
- And I'm starting to regret it.
- No. You're a good friend, and I owe you.

415
00:21:46,509 --> 00:21:47,760
I'm gonna find you
somebody to love.

416
00:21:47,885 --> 00:21:49,887
- No. You're not.
- Yes, I am.

417
00:21:50,137 --> 00:21:52,306
Trust me, there is no point
in going through life alone.

418
00:21:54,016 --> 00:21:55,434
Are you really
not gonna eat that?

419
00:22:05,110 --> 00:22:06,195
Thanks.

420
00:22:10,241 --> 00:22:12,952
Hello. Teresa. Agent Van Pelt.

421
00:22:13,410 --> 00:22:14,578
What brings you two here?

422
00:22:14,703 --> 00:22:17,832
Why didn't you tell us you called Frost
the week before he Killed Parkman?

423
00:22:17,957 --> 00:22:20,042
Look, there's nothing
suspicious about it.

424
00:22:20,167 --> 00:22:22,920
I was hired by Visualize
to protect Parkman from Frost.

425
00:22:23,045 --> 00:22:24,296
Bang-up job.

426
00:22:25,881 --> 00:22:27,633
Frost was threatening
a wrongful termination suit.

427
00:22:27,758 --> 00:22:29,343
I figured he was just
looking for a payout.

428
00:22:29,468 --> 00:22:32,012
- What'd you say to him?
- Well, I told him that Visualize's lawyers

429
00:22:32,137 --> 00:22:33,681
were behind Parkman,
and they'd keep it in the courts

430
00:22:33,806 --> 00:22:35,975
until Frost had spent
his last penny.

431
00:22:36,725 --> 00:22:39,728
What's wrong, Ray? These questions
making you uncomfortable?

432
00:22:41,730 --> 00:22:43,148
Do you see that?
It's that.

433
00:22:43,274 --> 00:22:44,316
It's a spider.

434
00:22:48,320 --> 00:22:49,572
I hate spiders.

435
00:22:50,781 --> 00:22:51,991
Better now?

436
00:22:55,619 --> 00:22:56,871
Look. It...

437
00:22:56,996 --> 00:23:00,249
It never occurred to me that Frost was
planning anything like a murder.

438
00:23:00,374 --> 00:23:02,042
He didn't really strike me
as the violent type.

439
00:23:04,003 --> 00:23:05,462
Live and learn, I guess.

440
00:23:05,963 --> 00:23:08,841
Sometimes you get it wrong.
Next time you do better.

441
00:23:10,217 --> 00:23:12,219
I've been trying to figure out
how Frost got away.

442
00:23:12,803 --> 00:23:14,388
You know, the trick that
Jane can't figure out.

443
00:23:14,513 --> 00:23:16,307
- And?
- Well, we know there was a third man.

444
00:23:16,432 --> 00:23:18,642
- Yeah, the one who killed Frost.
- There had to be a fourth as well.

445
00:23:18,767 --> 00:23:20,728
- An inside man.
- An inside man?

446
00:23:21,020 --> 00:23:23,397
Or woman.
A maid, a cook, a bellboy, whatever.

447
00:23:23,522 --> 00:23:26,275
Somebody who could help Frost get in
and out of the hotel without being seen.

448
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:29,778
There's no evidence Frost had contact
with any of the hotel's employees.

449
00:23:29,904 --> 00:23:32,740
- We did interviews.
- So? Somebody lied to you.

450
00:23:32,865 --> 00:23:35,743
The killer could have already been gone
by the time hotel security got to the room.

451
00:23:35,910 --> 00:23:38,370
All the witnesses said they heard a struggle
going on inside the room.

452
00:23:38,495 --> 00:23:40,998
Uh, there is a difference
between hearing and seeing.

453
00:23:41,123 --> 00:23:42,458
Autopsy report just came out.

454
00:23:43,167 --> 00:23:45,002
Parkman wasn't immediately
Killed from the knife wound.

455
00:23:45,127 --> 00:23:47,671
He died from a struggle
sometime after being stabbed.

456
00:23:48,422 --> 00:23:52,801
He had an aortal hemorrhage.
So... You're welcome.

457
00:23:58,098 --> 00:24:00,017
How did you get
the autopsy before us?

458
00:24:00,476 --> 00:24:02,478
I still have friends
in CBI, Teresa.

459
00:24:02,895 --> 00:24:04,396
They help when I need it.

460
00:24:06,941 --> 00:24:09,485
Speaking of...
helping friends

461
00:24:11,028 --> 00:24:13,572
a word of advice
for both of you.

462
00:24:13,697 --> 00:24:15,741
- Yeah? What's that?
- Jane.

463
00:24:16,951 --> 00:24:18,160
He's made a mess of things.

464
00:24:18,285 --> 00:24:19,995
He doesn't know when to leave
well enough alone.

465
00:24:20,120 --> 00:24:21,914
- You know what I mean?
- No, I don't.

466
00:24:23,123 --> 00:24:27,044
There's a lot of powerful people
that Jane's pissed off not just Red John.

467
00:24:28,379 --> 00:24:31,215
You should get out of the way, both of you
before something happens.

468
00:24:32,716 --> 00:24:36,178
- I really... I'd hate to see you get hurt.
- It's funny you should say that, Ray

469
00:24:36,303 --> 00:24:39,890
because, actually, we're putting your name
on a list of suspects for Parkman's murder.

470
00:24:40,015 --> 00:24:41,058
What? That's crazy.

471
00:24:41,183 --> 00:24:43,519
The third man could have killed
both Frost and Parkman.

472
00:24:44,311 --> 00:24:45,646
Frost killed Parkman.

473
00:24:45,771 --> 00:24:47,773
Maybe the third man just
wanted it to look that way.

474
00:24:47,898 --> 00:24:50,818
Maybe it's you, Ray. Maybe you're
the third man. You have motive.

475
00:24:57,032 --> 00:25:01,245
I just hope you remember this.
That I tried to help. Okay?

476
00:25:06,208 --> 00:25:08,085
I want to see that autopsy report.

477
00:25:12,006 --> 00:25:14,800
Thanks for coming in, detective Cordero.
I really appreciate it.

478
00:25:14,925 --> 00:25:16,343
Yeah. Call me Oscar.

479
00:25:17,553 --> 00:25:19,930
Wow. Nice digs you got here.

480
00:25:20,055 --> 00:25:22,307
No expense spared for you
CBI folks, huh?

481
00:25:22,599 --> 00:25:24,518
Yeah. Can I get you something?
Coffee?

482
00:25:24,643 --> 00:25:25,769
Nah, I'm fine.

483
00:25:26,687 --> 00:25:28,105
Here's the file
on the Kirkland shooting.

484
00:25:28,230 --> 00:25:30,607
It's got the prison report
plus my investigation.

485
00:25:30,733 --> 00:25:33,110
- Anything that stuck out?
- Nah.

486
00:25:33,736 --> 00:25:36,238
Only a sick puppy who couldn't
hack doing his time

487
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:39,199
looking for any way out...
in my opinion.

488
00:25:40,325 --> 00:25:42,411
And you spoke to the correctional officer
who shot him?

489
00:25:42,661 --> 00:25:44,872
Oh, yeah.
He was just a kid.

490
00:25:45,706 --> 00:25:49,209
Says your guy Kirkland paid no attention
to three verbal warnings, just kept going

491
00:25:49,334 --> 00:25:51,879
didn't even turn around.
Kid had to shoot.

492
00:25:52,421 --> 00:25:54,214
You hate to see something
like that, you know?

493
00:25:54,715 --> 00:25:59,803
Some piece of crap making a cop do
what he ain't got the stones to do himself.

494
00:25:59,928 --> 00:26:01,013
It ain't right.

495
00:26:01,555 --> 00:26:03,932
And I'll tell you somethin'.
Messed the kid up really bad.

496
00:26:04,600 --> 00:26:08,771
P.T.S.D... Or something.
It's a damn shame.

497
00:26:11,106 --> 00:26:13,108
- Can I make a copy of this?
- Don't need to.

498
00:26:13,233 --> 00:26:15,194
Brought that one for you.
Thought you might want it.

499
00:26:15,319 --> 00:26:18,030
- Thanks.
- Hey, no problem.

500
00:26:18,363 --> 00:26:20,574
Maybe one day you'll do
a favor for me, right?

501
00:26:28,040 --> 00:26:31,376
- He clear things up?
- Not really. I mean, he says it was clean.

502
00:26:31,502 --> 00:26:33,253
Kirkland was trying to escape,
nobody wanted to shoot him

503
00:26:33,378 --> 00:26:35,714
- but they were forced to... reluctantly.
- You don't buy it.

504
00:26:35,839 --> 00:26:38,092
Well, it says in here
he was shot six times in the back.

505
00:26:38,217 --> 00:26:40,344
- Six doesn't sound so reluctant.
- No, it doesn't.

506
00:26:41,261 --> 00:26:44,598
- That's the story we're gonna get.
- Okay. I'll tell the boss.

507
00:26:54,525 --> 00:26:57,694
Hi. Where can I find Kimball Cho?

508
00:26:58,153 --> 00:27:00,489
That's his desk, but he
stepped out for a moment.

509
00:27:00,614 --> 00:27:04,076
- Can I help you?
- Maybe. I met him this morning at the gym.

510
00:27:04,201 --> 00:27:07,412
I thought I'd come by and see if he wants
to let me buy him a cup of coffee.

511
00:27:07,830 --> 00:27:09,498
Plan seemed normal
when I was driving over here

512
00:27:09,623 --> 00:27:11,792
but now it's feeling
a little stalker-ish.

513
00:27:11,917 --> 00:27:16,046
- Does it seem stalker-ish to you?
- Mm, a little. Yeah.

514
00:27:16,171 --> 00:27:17,673
- Okay, I should go.
- Hey.

515
00:27:19,174 --> 00:27:21,468
- Remember me? Yeah.
- From the gym, yeah.

516
00:27:21,802 --> 00:27:23,929
- Come here. Sit down for a second.
- Darn ankle.

517
00:27:24,847 --> 00:27:25,973
Thanks.

518
00:27:29,560 --> 00:27:30,644
What are you doing here?

519
00:27:33,230 --> 00:27:35,482
My name's Kira.
Kira Tinsley.

520
00:27:35,983 --> 00:27:40,028
I was gonna buy you a coffee
but it was a bad idea. I'm gonna go.

521
00:27:40,154 --> 00:27:41,989
No, I can drink a cup of coffee.
Give me a second.

522
00:27:46,827 --> 00:27:50,330
When Parkman was stabbed
the knife glanced over the rib

523
00:27:50,455 --> 00:27:52,082
and barely nicked the aorta.

524
00:27:52,583 --> 00:27:55,669
Haffner wasn't lying.
There coroner says that...

525
00:27:56,128 --> 00:27:59,548
there was a struggle after the stabbing
that caused the nick to tear

526
00:27:59,673 --> 00:28:02,176
and that's what started the hemorrhaging
that Killed Parkman.

527
00:28:04,678 --> 00:28:07,973
And if Frost and Parkman
were really struggling

528
00:28:08,098 --> 00:28:09,224
and that's what caused
the hemorrhage

529
00:28:09,349 --> 00:28:12,978
how is it that Frost didn't get
Parkman's blood all over him?

530
00:28:13,103 --> 00:28:16,064
And how did he avoid tracking
bloody footprints out of the room?

531
00:28:20,110 --> 00:28:22,279
- Jane?
- I just solved the case.

532
00:28:22,487 --> 00:28:24,239
- Really? How?
- Yeah. But one thing first.

533
00:28:24,364 --> 00:28:25,449
Jane?

534
00:28:28,702 --> 00:28:31,997
Grace? Who was that woman
Cho just met with?

535
00:28:32,122 --> 00:28:33,582
Someone named Kira Tinsley.

536
00:28:33,707 --> 00:28:35,584
- Oh, the woman he met at the gym?
- That's right.

537
00:28:35,709 --> 00:28:38,754
- Attractive, right?
- She was okay, if you like that type.

538
00:28:39,213 --> 00:28:41,632
- You... you ever go to the gym, Grace?
- Sure.

539
00:28:41,798 --> 00:28:44,968
- You ever try to pick up a guy there?
- No.

540
00:28:45,510 --> 00:28:47,763
Did they happen to say
where they were going?

541
00:28:48,639 --> 00:28:49,723
Coffee.

542
00:28:49,848 --> 00:28:52,226
Now you just walk out
in the middle of a conversation?

543
00:28:52,351 --> 00:28:53,894
- Lisbon, can I ask a favor?
- What?

544
00:28:54,019 --> 00:28:57,147
Can you go get one of those
elastic exercise bands

545
00:28:57,272 --> 00:28:59,441
- the kind to help you stretch with?
- What do you need one of those for?

546
00:28:59,566 --> 00:29:01,526
Bring it to Parkman's hotel room.
I'll meet you there.

547
00:29:07,447 --> 00:29:11,243
What do I do?
Oh... Client relations. Boring stuff.

548
00:29:11,701 --> 00:29:13,161
Tell me more about the CBI.

549
00:29:14,621 --> 00:29:18,333
It's a job. Uh, we try to help people
Try to stop the bad guys.

550
00:29:18,458 --> 00:29:20,043
- You don't talk much, do you?
- No.

551
00:29:21,294 --> 00:29:22,337
It's all right.

552
00:29:22,587 --> 00:29:24,422
- I like that in a man.
- Good.

553
00:29:25,507 --> 00:29:27,425
- Hey, Cho.
- Hey.

554
00:29:27,550 --> 00:29:30,178
- Kira Tinsley, right?
- Yeah.

555
00:29:30,637 --> 00:29:32,472
Is that yours?
That looks good. May I?

556
00:29:32,597 --> 00:29:33,682
What?

557
00:29:35,934 --> 00:29:37,060
Nice muffin.

558
00:29:37,310 --> 00:29:39,688
I think this is yours.

559
00:29:42,774 --> 00:29:44,859
What is that?
I've never seen one of those.

560
00:29:44,985 --> 00:29:47,487
It's a bug, a listening device.

561
00:29:47,612 --> 00:29:49,990
Really? Because I found that
under Kimball's desk

562
00:29:50,115 --> 00:29:51,783
and I was certain
that it belonged to you.

563
00:29:51,908 --> 00:29:54,369
- Why would you say that?
- Because it goes with, uh...

564
00:29:56,788 --> 00:29:58,498
this. Here.

565
00:29:58,999 --> 00:30:00,625
And here's your wallet

566
00:30:00,750 --> 00:30:04,004
and that identifies you
as a private investigator, doesn't it?

567
00:30:04,254 --> 00:30:06,131
Yeah. All right. Okay.

568
00:30:07,048 --> 00:30:11,845
That's why I don't take jobs like this,
but... the client paid cash up front, so...

569
00:30:12,637 --> 00:30:15,015
- I'll take this. What?
- No, you can't... you can't have those.

570
00:30:15,140 --> 00:30:16,182
You can't...

571
00:30:17,392 --> 00:30:19,811
- Oops.
- That cost me 300 bucks.

572
00:30:20,186 --> 00:30:21,730
Yeah, I'm sorry.

573
00:30:24,399 --> 00:30:25,608
Excuse me a minute.

574
00:30:26,526 --> 00:30:28,153
Hey! Wait a minute.

575
00:30:28,653 --> 00:30:30,780
Look, it was just business.
Nothing personal, okay?

576
00:30:31,281 --> 00:30:33,742
- Your client...who was it?
- You know I can't reveal that.

577
00:30:33,867 --> 00:30:36,661
All right, I'm arresting you under
section 632. That's worth a year in jail.

578
00:30:36,786 --> 00:30:38,079
Hey, wait a second!
No, whoa, whoa, whoa!

579
00:30:38,204 --> 00:30:39,789
We can work something out
here, right?

580
00:30:40,790 --> 00:30:42,625
Jeez. What do you want to know?

581
00:30:42,959 --> 00:30:44,002
What were you hired to do?

582
00:30:44,711 --> 00:30:46,880
Bug your office, get close to you.

583
00:30:47,547 --> 00:30:49,591
Client wanted to know everything
you and your team knew.

584
00:30:49,716 --> 00:30:52,010
And who was your client?
Was it Visualize?

585
00:30:53,386 --> 00:30:55,930
Yeah. So are we good?

586
00:30:56,473 --> 00:30:58,600
- Are you Visualize?
- No.

587
00:30:58,975 --> 00:31:00,810
I just work for whoever hires me.

588
00:31:00,935 --> 00:31:02,729
- Are we done?
- Yeah, we're done.

589
00:31:04,272 --> 00:31:08,610
Look, you seem like a nice guy.
Uh... no hard feelings?

590
00:31:14,449 --> 00:31:17,494
Looks like she was hired by Visualize.
I'm gonna put a tail on her.

591
00:31:18,578 --> 00:31:19,746
No need.

592
00:31:20,497 --> 00:31:23,333
I put the bug in her bag.
We can listen to her whenever we like.

593
00:31:31,341 --> 00:31:33,385
Let's go solve that
double murder, shall we?

594
00:31:33,510 --> 00:31:34,552
Right.

595
00:31:43,395 --> 00:31:45,897
Teresa. Any idea why we're here?

596
00:31:46,815 --> 00:31:49,025
Your Mr. Jane asked us to come.

597
00:31:49,651 --> 00:31:51,236
Are you gonna teach us aerobics?

598
00:31:52,862 --> 00:31:54,489
Jane will be here any minute.

599
00:31:54,864 --> 00:31:58,368
So the gun was found
somewhere here, right?

600
00:31:59,452 --> 00:32:00,995
And the hotel is...

601
00:32:01,871 --> 00:32:03,373
- That way, yes?
- Right.

602
00:32:03,498 --> 00:32:05,542
You can't see it
but it's just the other side of that wall.

603
00:32:06,668 --> 00:32:08,586
- I'm right.
- About what?

604
00:32:08,711 --> 00:32:11,589
You stay here with Rigsby,
and keep very alert.

605
00:32:11,714 --> 00:32:13,299
I don't want anyone
getting hurt.

606
00:32:20,890 --> 00:32:22,642
Finally. We've been
waiting here long enough.

607
00:32:22,767 --> 00:32:24,686
All good things come
to those who wait.

608
00:32:25,395 --> 00:32:26,438
Thank you.

609
00:32:26,563 --> 00:32:28,731
You gonna solve the case now
with a stretchy band?

610
00:32:28,857 --> 00:32:31,109
Yes, among other things.

611
00:32:31,943 --> 00:32:33,862
The first thing that's
important to note

612
00:32:33,987 --> 00:32:35,989
is that Parkman and Frost
were lovers.

613
00:32:36,489 --> 00:32:39,742
- Gay? No, they weren't.
- Yes, they were.

614
00:32:39,868 --> 00:32:41,369
We never heard anything about that.

615
00:32:41,494 --> 00:32:45,248
No. Because the relationship started
when Parkman was Frost's coach.

616
00:32:45,373 --> 00:32:47,167
When Frost was still a minor.

617
00:32:47,750 --> 00:32:51,045
And I never felt I had to worry
about him being alone with her.

618
00:32:51,171 --> 00:32:54,382
Like I said, Parkman was Frost's mentor
since he was a teenager.

619
00:32:54,507 --> 00:32:56,050
And continued to the present day?

620
00:32:56,176 --> 00:32:59,471
Yes. Then the relationship
started going badly.

621
00:32:59,721 --> 00:33:02,182
When Parkman went to Europe,
that was the final straw.

622
00:33:03,016 --> 00:33:07,979
Mylar thought Frost was sneaking away
when Parkman wasn't looking.

623
00:33:08,104 --> 00:33:10,106
Parkman went to a gymnastics
tournament in Europe.

624
00:33:10,231 --> 00:33:12,400
In the past, Frost went with him.
This time, he didn't.

625
00:33:12,525 --> 00:33:15,653
He had it backwards.
Parkman hadn't invited Frost.

626
00:33:16,613 --> 00:33:19,741
So he assumed that he was being replaced
with a younger lover...

627
00:33:20,200 --> 00:33:22,285
and decided to seek revenge.

628
00:33:22,410 --> 00:33:24,954
So he tried filing
a wrongful termination suit

629
00:33:25,079 --> 00:33:28,333
but he found himself being
intimidated by Visualize.

630
00:33:30,919 --> 00:33:32,837
So he tried the hard touch

631
00:33:32,962 --> 00:33:35,089
when Parkman came to the hotel
for the conference.

632
00:33:35,215 --> 00:33:38,468
"Now their relationship had
always been a consensual one"

633
00:33:38,927 --> 00:33:42,388
"but that wouldn't matter
in the eyes of the public or the law."

634
00:33:42,514 --> 00:33:45,099
"It was in Frost's power
to transform Parkman"

635
00:33:45,225 --> 00:33:49,896
"from a... an Olympic coach to a child
molester with just a few words."

636
00:33:50,021 --> 00:33:52,357
"He demanded that Parkman
bring a large sum of money"

637
00:33:52,482 --> 00:33:54,484
"across the park
in a brown paper bag."

638
00:33:54,609 --> 00:33:57,612
"Or Frost would ruin
Parkman's life forever."

639
00:33:57,737 --> 00:34:01,824
"And Frost was right in his estimation
of what Parkman stood to lose."

640
00:34:02,742 --> 00:34:05,537
What he didn't realize was
how Parkman would react.

641
00:34:11,876 --> 00:34:13,962
"The soda bottle
silencer worked perfectly."

642
00:34:14,087 --> 00:34:16,089
"Even if it did blow off,
no one heard a thing."

643
00:34:16,548 --> 00:34:20,885
"But Parkman hadn't expected
that Frost would have a knife."

644
00:34:21,386 --> 00:34:23,096
"The wound looked to be minor"

645
00:34:23,221 --> 00:34:26,724
"so Parkman put his gun back in his bag
and he left Frost."

646
00:34:26,849 --> 00:34:29,227
"Already Parkman was
starting to panic."

647
00:34:30,395 --> 00:34:32,355
"What happened in the car
hadn't gone to plan."

648
00:34:32,480 --> 00:34:34,357
"Nothing was going to plan."

649
00:34:35,567 --> 00:34:37,277
Whoa! What the heck!

650
00:34:37,402 --> 00:34:38,861
And when you arrived

651
00:34:38,987 --> 00:34:41,823
Parkman was trying to figure out
what to do with the gun.

652
00:34:42,407 --> 00:34:45,410
The window was his only chance.
He couldn't just throw it out there, though.

653
00:34:45,535 --> 00:34:48,246
It would end up right down there
in the street.

654
00:34:48,871 --> 00:34:51,874
But... If he could just

655
00:34:52,166 --> 00:34:53,668
- "Mr. Parkman?"
- Get it...

656
00:34:54,502 --> 00:34:56,963
a little... Further.

657
00:34:58,506 --> 00:35:02,218
Just that little... bit...

658
00:35:03,052 --> 00:35:04,220
Further.

659
00:35:11,603 --> 00:35:12,687
What the hell?

660
00:35:14,022 --> 00:35:16,899
"When the gun landed on the ground
in the alley, it went off."

661
00:35:17,025 --> 00:35:19,402
"That was the gunshot
that witnesses heard."

662
00:35:19,527 --> 00:35:20,570
TV remote.

663
00:35:21,613 --> 00:35:25,158
Very likely we'll find a bullet hole
somewhere in the alley.

664
00:35:25,867 --> 00:35:28,661
But who Killed Parkman?
Where'd all the blood come from?

665
00:35:29,287 --> 00:35:32,165
When Parkman
pulled on the elastic...

666
00:35:32,457 --> 00:35:33,958
his aorta ruptured.

667
00:35:42,216 --> 00:35:43,301
Fore!

668
00:35:45,178 --> 00:35:46,387
What the hell?

669
00:35:51,267 --> 00:35:52,352
Help.

670
00:35:55,396 --> 00:35:56,773
He stabbed me.

671
00:35:59,692 --> 00:36:02,070
So you're telling me that nobody
came in or out of the room.

672
00:36:02,987 --> 00:36:04,697
It was just Mr. Parkman
the whole time?

673
00:36:05,156 --> 00:36:06,491
Yeah.

674
00:36:06,616 --> 00:36:08,368
The trick was
that there was no trick.

675
00:36:11,621 --> 00:36:12,872
Et voilà.

676
00:36:18,192 --> 00:36:20,987
Good-bye, agent Lisbon,
Mr. Jane.

677
00:36:21,988 --> 00:36:26,117
I hope you won't be insulted when I say
that I hope I won't be seeing you again.

678
00:36:26,242 --> 00:36:27,451
Oh, we won't be.

679
00:36:27,577 --> 00:36:30,079
I'm sure Mr. Haffner would say
thank you if he were here.

680
00:36:30,204 --> 00:36:34,083
Where is he? It's not like him to leave
without one last barbed compliment.

681
00:36:34,208 --> 00:36:36,460
Oh, he had some business
to attend to.

682
00:36:36,752 --> 00:36:38,671
I am not his only client.

683
00:36:40,256 --> 00:36:43,009
I was hoping your office
could be discreet.

684
00:36:43,593 --> 00:36:45,261
- You know what I mean.
- Yes.

685
00:36:49,557 --> 00:36:52,560
Speaking... of discreet

686
00:36:53,227 --> 00:36:56,063
it was stupid of you
to try and bug the CBI.

687
00:36:56,188 --> 00:36:57,231
What are you talking about?

688
00:36:57,356 --> 00:37:01,277
Hiring someone outside Visualize
might have made you feel safe

689
00:37:01,902 --> 00:37:04,071
but she admitted
that you hired her.

690
00:37:04,196 --> 00:37:05,573
Agent Cho told me.

691
00:37:07,199 --> 00:37:08,951
I don't know what you're talking about.

692
00:37:09,076 --> 00:37:12,705
But all I can say is that if someone
did bug you it wasn't me.

693
00:37:14,540 --> 00:37:15,916
Bye, agent Lisbon.

694
00:37:19,545 --> 00:37:21,631
Jane? Are you okay?

695
00:37:22,840 --> 00:37:26,844
- Grace needs to trace this immediately.
- Why? What's going on?

696
00:37:27,011 --> 00:37:29,138
Cooper wasn't lying.
He didn't bug the CBI.

697
00:37:29,263 --> 00:37:32,725
- Well, who did?
- Red John. We need to find Kira Tinsley.

698
00:37:32,850 --> 00:37:35,936
She either works for Red John, or she's
about to become his next victim.

699
00:37:39,732 --> 00:37:41,776
"She's in the north section
of the city, boss."

700
00:37:41,901 --> 00:37:43,402
"Van Pelt's working
on the address."

701
00:37:44,987 --> 00:37:46,739
No answer on her cell,
and the landline's busy.

702
00:37:46,864 --> 00:37:48,699
- Yeah, we know.
- "Yeah, you say 9:00 to 6:00"

703
00:37:48,824 --> 00:37:50,493
"and then you never even show up."

704
00:37:50,785 --> 00:37:51,869
Almost got it.

705
00:37:51,994 --> 00:37:54,372
No. I paid my cleaning lady
to stay here all day

706
00:37:54,497 --> 00:37:56,415
to let the guy in,
and then he never came.

707
00:37:56,540 --> 00:37:57,583
Okay.

708
00:37:57,750 --> 00:37:59,418
1065 Oak Terrace.

709
00:37:59,543 --> 00:38:01,379
We're on Douglas,
headed toward Folsom.

710
00:38:01,504 --> 00:38:05,299
Yeah. No, I want my cable
fixed now, tonight or I'm gonna...

711
00:38:06,217 --> 00:38:08,886
I'm g... yeah, hold on a second.
Somebody's here.

712
00:38:14,892 --> 00:38:17,561
I need SAC P.D. to 1065 Oak Terrace
as fast as they can.

713
00:38:18,229 --> 00:38:19,522
Okay, I'm coming!

714
00:38:27,488 --> 00:38:28,614
Oh, hi.

715
00:38:29,448 --> 00:38:31,867
Okay, you're about ten minutes away.
We'll direct you.

716
00:38:31,992 --> 00:38:33,202
Could you drive faster?

717
00:38:34,870 --> 00:38:36,205
- "Help."
- Oh, crap.

718
00:38:37,957 --> 00:38:39,625
Boss, you're gonna want to
take the next left...

719
00:38:39,750 --> 00:38:40,835
and then a right on El Camino.

720
00:38:47,425 --> 00:38:48,843
Got a patrol car
six blocks away.

721
00:39:11,615 --> 00:39:13,033
Police! I'm coming in.

722
00:39:14,452 --> 00:39:15,661
SAC P.D.'s on scene.

723
00:39:19,498 --> 00:39:21,584
Come on. Oh, come on,
stay with me.

724
00:39:21,709 --> 00:39:23,627
Stay with me. You're gonna be okay.
Come on, stay with me.

725
00:39:23,753 --> 00:39:24,962
Right on El Camino, boss.

726
00:39:26,922 --> 00:39:29,300
- Damn it.
- Just drive, Lisbon.

727
00:39:31,635 --> 00:39:34,430
"I need E.M.T.S.
I need medical assistance immediately."

728
00:39:43,522 --> 00:39:45,274
Now look at me.
You're gonna be fine, okay?

729
00:39:45,399 --> 00:39:46,942
They're on their way.
Wait...

730
00:39:47,401 --> 00:39:49,862
Where are the E.M.T.S?
We need E.M.T.S.

731
00:39:49,987 --> 00:39:52,448
I'm CBI. Who did this to you?

732
00:39:54,283 --> 00:39:55,409
Tell me.

733
00:39:57,203 --> 00:39:58,537
Tattoo...

734
00:40:01,457 --> 00:40:06,462
Tattoo on his left arm.

735
00:40:42,331 --> 00:40:43,499
What did she say?

736
00:40:45,668 --> 00:40:49,046
She told me Red John has a tattoo
on his left shoulder.

737
00:40:49,547 --> 00:40:50,840
Three dots.

738
00:40:52,049 --> 00:40:54,510
That's my leverage. I got him.


